  "design_purpose": "The Foundry is the point system (docs/systems/progression-eras.md, D-019): points are INGOTS placed on a forged PLATE, and power comes from arrangement, not from the ingot's number. Every ingot is one verb (a modifier from items.json at a flat, small value that never changes). Two orthogonally adjacent ingots that match a pair make that pair's mechanic (Ember beside Reach: wildfire spreads further); a straight line of line_length matching ingots adds the ingot's modifier again at line_bonus times its value (the set effect). Ingots come from milestones and knowledge, never kills-per-se: sources map an event to one ingot, each once - the first workbench, the first smelt, each family's first kill, the mine reinforced, the Tyrant's fall, the era itself, the first alloy; era gates when a source may grant, so the total available is capped per era and the cap rises with the world. plate_by_era forges the plate a row wider per era (3x3, 3x4, 4x4): space pressure arrives mid-game, not at the start. Re-forging (taking an ingot off the plate) pays reforge_cost, cheap early, so layout is a habit not a commitment. Numbers stay within the threefold budget: ten ingots placed with two pairs is roughly +50% in one element and two mechanics, not a new sheet."
